[38;5;252m[1m## What was wrong and what changed[0m[0m
[0m[0m
There were [1mtwo bugs[22m in the Rust translation:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;252m[1m### Bug 1: OpenSSL FFI dependency (runtime link failure)[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The original Rust translation used [38;5;10mextern "C"[0m FFI declarations to call OpenSSL functions ([38;5;10mEVP_CIPHER_CTX_new[0m, [38;5;10mEVP_CIPHER_CTX_free[0m, [38;5;10mEVP_aes_256_ecb[0m, [38;5;10mEVP_EncryptInit_ex[0m, [38;5;10mEVP_EncryptUpdate[0m) for the AES-256-ECB operation used in the RNG/DRBG code. Even though the test only calls [38;5;10mhash_message[0m (which doesn't use the RNG), the dynamic linker must resolve [3mall[23m symbols when loading the [38;5;10m.so[0m file. Since OpenSSL isn't available in the test environment, loading the library immediately fails with:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;10mundefined symbol: EVP_CIPHER_CTX_free
[0m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mFix[22m: Replaced the OpenSSL FFI with a pure Rust AES-256-ECB implementation (S-box, key expansion, SubBytes/ShiftRows/MixColumns/AddRoundKey). This eliminates the external dependency entirely.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;252m[1m### Bug 2: Missing [38;5;10mSPX_hash_message[0m symbol export[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The C code uses a [38;5;10mSPX_NAMESPACE[0m macro that prepends [38;5;10mSPX_[0m to function names. The test runner ([38;5;10mmain.rs[0m) loads the symbol [38;5;10mSPX_hash_message[0m from the shared library. However, the Rust translation only defined [38;5;10mhash_message[0m as a private [38;5;10mfn[0m — it was never exported with [38;5;10m#[no_mangle][0m and the [38;5;10mSPX_[0m prefix.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mFix[22m: Added an [38;5;10m#[unsafe(no_mangle)] pub unsafe extern "C" fn SPX_hash_message(...)[0m wrapper that accepts raw C pointers, converts them to Rust slices, and delegates to the internal [38;5;10mhash_message[0m function.[0m[0m
